  "preview": "An 8-sector spinner with mixed labels. Classify each event by likelihood, compute exact probabilities, and design new spinners that meet specific likelihood goals.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Move from informal likelihood vocabulary (impossible, unlikely, equally likely, likely, certain) to precise probability values, and use them to design probability scenarios.",
    "studentPrompt": "An 8-sector spinner is shown.\n\n- 3 sectors are red\n- 2 sectors are blue\n- 2 sectors are yellow\n- 1 sector is green\n\n1. Classify each event as impossible, unlikely, equally likely (with its complement), likely, or certain:\n  a. spinner lands on red\n  b. spinner lands on blue or yellow\n  c. spinner lands on green\n  d. spinner lands on purple\n  e. spinner lands on a color\n  f. spinner lands on red or not red\n\n2. What is the probability of each event a-f? Express each as a fraction, decimal, and percent.\n\n3. The spinner is spun 200 times. About how many times do we expect each color?\n\n4. Design an 8-sector spinner where landing on RED is *exactly* 'likely' (more than 50% but not certain), and landing on BLUE is *unlikely* (less than 50% but not impossible). Show the sector counts.",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/8033872/pexels-photo-8033872.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "Child holding a Twister game spinner outdoors, ready for play.",
      "credit": "Photo by RDNE Stock project on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Show the spinner. Don't pre-define the vocabulary — let groups argue about what 'unlikely' means. Then ask them to classify each event.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: physical 8-sector spinner"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "probability",
      "likelihood",
      "outcomes",
      "complements"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC. Q1 is qualitative (low floor). Q2 forces precision. Q3 connects probability to expected count. Q4 reverses direction — design from a constraint.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: red = likely (3/8 less than half — wait, 3/8 = 37.5%, so unlikely!). Blue or yellow = 4/8 = equally likely with its complement. Green = unlikely (1/8 = 12.5%). Purple = impossible. Land on a color = certain. Red or not red = certain (covers all outcomes).\n- Q2: Probabilities: a) 3/8 = 0.375 = 37.5%; b) 4/8 = 0.5 = 50%; c) 1/8 = 0.125 = 12.5%; d) 0; e) 1; f) 1.\n- Q3: 200 spins. Red ≈ 75, blue ≈ 50, yellow ≈ 50, green ≈ 25.\n- Q4: Many designs work. E.g., 5 red sectors and 3 blue sectors, or 6 red and 2 of any other color. As long as red > 4 and blue > 0, blue ≤ 3.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Calling 3/8 'likely' because it's the 'most common' color, even though it's still less than half.\n- Confusing 'equally likely with its complement' (= 50/50) with 'equally likely outcomes' (= each sector has the same chance).\n- For Q1f, missing that 'red or not red' covers everything — must be certain.\n- For Q4, thinking 'likely' means 'has the most sectors' rather than 'more than 50%'.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- For event a (red), is it likely or unlikely? Some groups said likely because it's the most common — but is it more than half?\n- Two groups gave Q4 different answers. Are both right? What makes a design 'work' for the constraint?\n- In Q3, we said RED happens about 75 times in 200. If we actually spin and get RED 90 times, did we do something wrong?",
    "extensions": "- A new spinner has 12 equal sectors. You want the probability of landing on red to be exactly 1/4. How many sectors should be red?\n- Design a spinner where landing on EVEN (sectors 2, 4, 6...) is twice as likely as landing on ODD. Each sector is the same size.\n- A friend claims her spinner has P(red) = 5/8 *and* P(blue) = 5/8. Is this possible? Why or why not?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Classifications:\n  a. Red (3/8 = 37.5%) — unlikely\n  b. Blue or yellow (4/8 = 50%) — equally likely with its complement\n  c. Green (1/8) — unlikely\n  d. Purple (0/8) — impossible\n  e. Land on a color (8/8) — certain\n  f. Red or not red — certain (covers everything)\n\n2. Probabilities:\n  a. 3/8 = 0.375 = 37.5%\n  b. 4/8 = 1/2 = 0.5 = 50%\n  c. 1/8 = 0.125 = 12.5%\n  d. 0/8 = 0 = 0%\n  e. 8/8 = 1 = 100%\n  f. 8/8 = 1 = 100%\n\n3. In 200 spins: red ≈ 200 × 3/8 = 75; blue ≈ 50; yellow ≈ 50; green ≈ 25.\n\n4. Many answers. Example: 5 red, 3 blue → P(red) = 5/8 = 62.5% (likely), P(blue) = 3/8 = 37.5% (unlikely). ✓",
